# Test fixtures are compared byte for byte, so their line endings are part of the
# fixture. Without this, a Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true` — which is
# what the GitHub Windows runner and a default Git for Windows install both do —
# rewrites every LF to CRLF, and a test that asserts a rendered template equals a
# string written with `\n` fails on Windows and nowhere else. The frontmatter
# parser these fixtures feed returns a body verbatim on purpose (see the
# `crlf_line_endings_parse` unit test in src/skills.rs), so the fix belongs at the
# checkout boundary rather than in the parser.
tests/fixtures/** text eol=lf

# The cross-version fixture databases are BINARY, and the rule above would
# otherwise treat them as text: a SQLite page whose bytes happen to contain
# `0D 0A` gets rewritten to `0A` on `git add`, which silently corrupts the file
# the moment one is written whose content lands on that pair. The 0.22.0
# fixtures survived only because none of their pages contained it; the 0.29.0
# ones did on their first write, which is how this was found.
tests/fixtures/**/*.sqlite3 binary
